    body:`
<h2>The goal is to beat the dealer, not chase 21</h2><p>A hand closer to 21 than the dealer wins, provided it does not go over. An 18 beats 17; even 12 wins when the dealer busts. I think dropping the “I must make 21” idea is the fastest beginner upgrade.</p><p>Over 21 is a <strong>bust</strong> and loses immediately. Equal final totals are a <strong>push</strong>, so the original bet is normally returned. No win, no damage.</p><div class="callout"><strong>Table talk:</strong> 20 is already a monster hand. Hitting it because “an ace might be next” is not brave—it is basically speedrunning a bust.</div>
<h2>Blackjack card values</h2><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Card</th><th>Value</th><th>Key detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>2–10</strong></td><td>Face value</td><td>A seven is 7; a ten is 10</td></tr><tr><td><strong>J, Q, K</strong></td><td>10</td><td>All face cards are equal</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Ace</strong></td><td>1 or 11</td><td>The useful value is applied automatically</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>An ace acts like a shock absorber. A+6 is soft 17. Add a ten and the ace drops from 11 to 1, leaving 17 instead of a bust. This clicks much faster in a live example than in a wall of theory.</p><div class="compare-grid"><div class="compare-card"><b>Hard hand</b><span>No ace counted as 11. Examples: 10+6, or A+5+10, are hard 16.</span></div><div class="compare-card"><b>Soft hand</b><span>An ace still counts as 11. A+7 is soft 18, so one extra card cannot bust it.</span></div></div>
<h2>How a round works</h2><ol><li>The player places a practice bet and receives two cards.</li><li>The dealer receives two cards, usually one face up and one face down.</li><li>The player acts using their cards and the dealer's upcard.</li><li>The dealer reveals the hole card and follows the table's fixed drawing rule.</li><li>Remaining hands are compared.</li></ol><p>The player acts first and can bust before the dealer moves. That order is a core source of the house edge. The dealer does not improvise or “feel lucky”; the rule printed on the table runs the show.</p>
<h2>Hit, Stand, Double, Split, and Surrender</h2><div class="myths"><div class="myth"><b>Hit</b><span>Take one more card. Repeat until you stand or bust.</span></div><div class="myth"><b>Stand</b><span>Keep the current total and end your turn.</span></div><div class="myth"><b>Double</b><span>Double the bet, receive exactly one card, then stand automatically.</span></div><div class="myth"><b>Split</b><span>Turn a matching pair into two separately funded hands.</span></div></div><p><strong>Surrender</strong> gives up the hand and usually returns half the bet, when offered. Insurance is a separate side bet available against a dealer ace. I would skip it as a beginner: the name sounds comforting, but the maths does not make it a safety net.</p>
<h2>Natural blackjack versus a regular 21</h2><p>A <strong>blackjack</strong> is specifically an ace plus a ten, jack, queen, or king as the first two cards. A+K is blackjack; 7+7+7 is simply 21. After splitting aces, A+10 is commonly treated as a regular 21. Always check the table rules.</p><p>Blackjack typically beats a multi-card 21 and receives a special payout. If both player and dealer have blackjack, the result is normally a push. “Even money” against a dealer ace is insurance wearing a different hat—not free value.</p>
<h2>Dealer rules: S17 and H17</h2><p>The dealer must hit 16 or less and stand on hard 17 or more. The fork is soft 17, such as A+6. <strong>S17</strong> means stand; <strong>H17</strong> means hit. H17 is slightly worse for the player.</p><div class="formula">S17 = dealer stands on soft 17 · H17 = dealer hits soft 17</div><p>I always check the felt before practising a chart. Deck count, double-after-split, surrender, and soft-17 rules can change the best decision. A basic-strategy chart without its rules is a map with no scale.</p>
<h2>Why 3:2 beats 6:5</h2><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Typical payout</th><th>Profit on 100</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Regular win</strong></td><td>1:1</td><td>100</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Blackjack 3:2</strong></td><td>1.5 to 1</td><td>150</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Blackjack 6:5</strong></td><td>1.2 to 1</td><td>120</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Push</strong></td><td>Bet returned</td><td>0</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>That 30-unit gap adds up. I consider 3:2 the sensible benchmark; 6:5 is a meaningful downgrade. The big sign says blackjack, but the small print sets the maths.</p>
<h2>A sample hand, step by step</h2><p>You hold 10+6 against a dealer 10: hard 16. Standing feels rough, but hitting can bust. Under common multi-deck rules, basic strategy says Hit because standing performs even worse on average.</p><p>A 4 arrives, making 20, so I stand. The dealer reveals 6, must hit 16, draws 8, and busts. If I had drawn a king and busted instead, the decision would not become wrong. A move is judged by its expected result before the card lands, not by one heater or one brick.</p>
<h2>Five beginner leaks</h2><ul><li><strong>Playing only against 21.</strong> You are playing against the dealer's final hand.</li><li><strong>Copying the dealer.</strong> You have more choices and the disadvantage of acting first.</li><li><strong>Buying insurance by reflex.</strong> It is another wager, not protection for the first one.</li><li><strong>Ignoring 3:2 versus 6:5.</strong> The payout changes the game's maths.</li><li><strong>Results-based thinking.</strong> One win does not prove a bad move was good.</li></ul><div class="callout"><strong>My take:</strong> learn the mechanics, then drill basic strategy under one clear ruleset. Betting systems do not repair bad decisions or erase the house edge. Keep it calm; no chasing, no “this one is due” nonsense.</div>`,
